> > 2.1 Most methods based on population genetics assume that loci are independent of each other, even if they don't acknowledge it directly. In other words, models assume that loci are inherited by offspring as independent units from their parents (due to random mating, independent assortment of chromosomes and recombination). This is not always true. For example, search for "linked selection". That's why it does not matter what allele is the reference and which is the alternative. Also, that's why it is important to filter loci in linkage disequilibrium. By the way, early next week, we will have ready a nice function to filter loci in LD. It is also important to read and understand the theory behind the method you are using and the question you are trying to answer. For example, filtering on LD should not be used to estimate heterozygosity see for example:
> > - Dementieva N, Mitrofanova O, Dysin A, Kudinov A, Stanishevskaya O, Larkina T, et al. Assessing the effects of rare alleles and linkage disequilibrium on estimates of genetic diversity in the chicken populations. Animal. 2021;15(3):100171.
> > - Schmidt TL, Jasper ME, Weeks AR, Hoffmann AA. Unbiased population heterozygosity estimates from genome‐wide sequence data. Methods in Ecology and Evolution. 2021;12(10):1888-98.
> >
> > 2.2 You could also randomly change the allocation of 0's and 2's in a genlight object using the function gl.random.snp() and replicate your analysis and double-check that you obtain the same results.
